save valuable human lives from a possible terrorist attack. UAVs are greatly sought after in non-military applications as well. At times of industrial accidents or life-threatening situations, they can provide rescue teams with critical close-up information that will help the rescue teams perform better in their operations. For example, UAVs equipped with infrared cameras are able to locate trapped victims in burning buildings. They are able to manoeuvre through buildings to find sources of dangerous toxins or chemicals at the sites of industrial accidents. In the current work, the emphasis is on the use of artificial intelligence to automate the design of a smallscale quadrotor UAV/MAV. Rotary-wing UAV/MAV has many strong advantages, such as the ability to hover at a stationary spot, and manoeuvre in densely populated areas with buildings in close proximity. A low-cost approach to the development of an UAV can be achieved by integrating the smallest commercial-off-the-shelf components (such as propulsion systems, sensors, power source, video transmitter, and video camera) available. However, faced with a wide range of components from different vendors, it is a daunting task to select the best combination of components, and position them in order to obtain the most compact vehicle, and still satisfy the physical and mission constraints. This work
