4 GENETIC ALGORITHMS
GA consist of search procedures that mimic the natural biological evolution (Holland [12]), with many improvements contributed subsequently (De Jong [13]; Goldberg [14]). GA operate on a population of potential solutions, applying the ‘survival of the fittest’ principle to produce better approximations to a solution. Solutions in GA are represented as chromosomes with the most common representation using a single-level, binary string. Studies done (Wright [15]) have shown that realvalued representation in GA offers numerous advantages such as better efficiency and lesser computer memory usage. Thus, real-valued representation is used in this study. Each of the design parameters is represented by a real number and the chromosome is an array of these design parameters, rather than a binary string. The parameters have their corresponding upper and lower bounds of allowable values. One obvious advantage is the reduction of overheads incurred in the conversion of the binary number into the real number. In the current study, the chromosome is a string of mixed combination of integer and float numbers. The objective function in the GA optimization problem is defined as
