Crossover is the main genetic search operator in GA. It recombines the genetic material in two parent chromosomes to create two children chromosomes. The new individuals possess some parts of genetic material from both the parent chromosomes. It is a powerful process that extends the search in many directions. The crossover operation is controlled by a crossover rate, denoted by Pc, which is usually set at 80 to 100 per cent. Intermediate crossover is adopted in this study as studies done (Muhlenbein and Schlierkamp-Voosen [16]) have revealed promising results. Mutation serves as a secondary search operator for exploring new search region by altering the allele of a
Fig. 6 Flow chart of design optimization using GA
gene randomly. For real-valued chromosomes, mutation is achieved by perturbing the gene values within the allowed range. This operation helps to ensure that the search process will not be trapped in local minima. The probability (or mutation rate), denoted by Pm, of the mutation process is usually set low, typically around 5 to 20 per cent. It is not advisable to set Pm too high, as the optimization process will tend towards a haphazard and random search. There is no standard criterion for terminating the search process. It really depends on the amount of time the designer is willing to wait to obtain the near-optimal or exact optimal solutions. In this study, the termination criterion is set to be the point when the improvement in objective value is less than 0.5 per cent for consecutive 100 000 generation. The optimization design flowchart using GA is shown in Fig. 6.
